===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|polynyi|pl2qual=rare}}

# {{lb|en|hydrology|oceanography}} A [[naturally]] [[form#Verb|formed]] [[transient]] [[area]] of [[open water]] [[surround#Verb|surrounded]] by [[sea ice]], especially in [[polar]] or [[subpolar]] seas. {{defdate|from mid 19th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|glade}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Ferdinand von Wrangel|Ferdinand von Wrangell}}|chapter=V|editor=w:Edward Sabine|title=Narrative of an Expedition to the Polar Sea {{nb...|in the Years 1820, 1821, 1822 & 1823. Commanded by Lieutenant, now Admiral Ferdinand von Wrangell, of the Russian Imperial Navy.}}|edition=2nd|location=London|publisher=James Madden and Co.,{{nb...|8, Leadenhall Street.}}|year=1844|pages=102–103|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=m-MDAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA103|oclc=317512813|passage=We immediately ascended a hill, and saw that the supposed land was nothing but hummocks of ice, piled up beyond a large '''Polynia''', or space of open water, which extended from east to west, as far as the eye could reach.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Polar Seas and Sir {{w|John Franklin}}|magazine=w:Putnam's Magazine|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:G. P. Putnam's Sons|GP Putnam & Co.]] {{nb...|10 Park Place.}}; London: [[w:Sampson Low|Sampson Low, Son & Co.]]|month=June|year=1853|volume=I|issue=VI|page=635|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ICE0AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA635|column=2|oclc=221113463|passage=Hoping to reach the starting-place in the early season of navigation, he intends to follow his course of travel nearly upon a meridional line, which would, it is believed, lead him to the '''Polynya'''—a ''mare liberum'', or such, comparatively speaking—within its formidable borderings of the thick-ribbed ice.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Summary of the Meeting|title=Polar Bears {{nb...|Proceedings of the Seventh Working Meeting of the IUCN Polar Bear Specialist Group Held at the Artisk Institut, Copenhagen, Denmark, 30 January&nbsp;– 1 February 1979 and Proceedings of the Sixth Working Meeting of the IUCN Polar Bear Specialist Group Held at IUCN Headquarters, Switzerland, 7–10 December 1976}}|location=Gland, Switzerland|publisher=[[w:International Union for Conservation of Nature|International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources]]|year=1980|section=paragraph 9|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=z784DCYBrZsC&pg=PA23|isbn=978-2-88032-206-9|passage=Dr. [[w:Ian Stirling (biologist)|[Ian] Stirling]] pointed out that colonies of nesting sea-birds were an indicator of permanent '''polynias''' and that the productivity studies in such '''polynias''' might prove interesting.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=George A. Knox|chapter=The Southern Ocean|title=Biology of the Southern Ocean|edition=2nd|series=Marine Biology Series|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher=w:CRC Press|year=2006|section=section 1.6|page=11|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lF_LBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA11|column=1|isbn=978-0-8493-3394-1|passage='''Polynas''' play an important role in heat transfer from the oceans to the atmosphere, ice production, the formation of dense shelf water, spring disintegration of sea ice, phytoplankton and zooplankton production, and the distribution of higher trophic animals such as cephalopods, fish, birds, seals, and cetaceans.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jane George|title=‘We’ve had the biggest surprises and more questions are coming out.’: The secret life of snowy owls|url=https://nunatsiaq.com/stories/article/Weve_had_the_biggest_surprises_and_more_questions_are_coming_out/|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20181229204314/https://nunatsiaq.com/stories/article/Weve_had_the_biggest_surprises_and_more_questions_are_coming_out/|archivedate=29 December 2018|newspaper=w:Nunatsiaq News|date=20 June 2008|passage=Researchers now plan to compare their routes with satellite images and see whether the owls stayed around the '''polynas''', where snowy owls have been seen, picking off eiders swimming in the open water.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2022|author={{w|Thomas Halliday (writer)|Thomas Halliday}}|title=Otherlands|page=237|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2023
|passage=Below the glacier, within the '''polynya''', two rivers flow, a river of salt and a river of earth.}}

